utunnels,
OMG! You are right! I put a stop SE event command right before the SE plays and it seems to have fixed it! Just did a test and 20 out of 20 times, the SE played!
Thank you so much for the suggestion!
Going over the rest of the suggestions now. I realized after reading through the thread and caethyril's and bass2yang's latest comments that there is a common denominator to the instances where the SE "blips" during cutscenes; it's always and only cutscenes where there is currently no BGM playing...same deal with the RPG Maker Sound Test tool, i'll usually have all other music and everything disabled when I'm using it so I can hear the SEs better.
I'm going to try something a little tricksy and play a silent BGM on these events so that Windows has "something" audio related processing and see how that effects the issue.
Edit:
Just did 20 test runs of a cutscene that fairly frequently had the sound blipping out (the one I posted about in the original post). With a silent BGM (i.e. 0 volume) playing at the start of the event, no SE blips occured in all 20 tests!
I think you all may very well be right, it's something to do with Windows and the way it's processing sounds in RPG Maker. Going to do more thorough testing over the next week as 20 test runs isn't enough for me to say the issue is totally solved, but I am tentatively hoping this works to prevent the sound issue, with this silent BGM method...a little bit of a rough way to do it, but if it works, it works!
Note that this testing also included 20 more tests of a New Game being fired, and in all 20 again, the SE played as it should, with the Stop SE event command played right before the Decision SE. So I think that is working perfectly.
Thank you all so much for the assistance, I've been trying to solve this issue for over a year now and I think I may finally be approaching a method to do so!!! I will post again in this thread if I am able to get the bug to reappear even with the above methods, need much more testing to truly conclude the issue as solved.
